{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NTUxogJMD8WvfyiEFc2DTt","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tPROMPT WORKFLOW (TASK -> SLOT-BASED STRUCTURED PROMPT)\n2\t\n3\tYou will read a free-form TASK_FILE and produce one normalized prompt document\n4\twith optional CONTEXT plus a fixed SLOTS + CONSTRAINTS layout.\n5\t\n6\tInputs (absolute paths provided):\n7\t- TASK_FILE: {{TASK_FILE}}\n8\t- INPUT_DIR (optional): {{INPUT_DIR}}         # Not used for evidence in this workflow\n9\t- SESSION_DIR: {{SESSION_DIR}}\n10\t- FORMAT_RULES: {{FORMAT_RULES_PATH}}        # rules/format.session.md (style only)\n11\t- AUDIT_RULES: {{AUDIT_RULES_PATH}}          # Internal check discipline only\n12\t- MANIFEST: {{SESSION_DIR}}/manifest.json    # CLI-provided manifest (authoritative I/O)\n13\t\n14\tGoal:\n15\tProduce ONE markdown file representing the structured prompt.\n16\tOutput filename is controlled by MANIFEST:\n17\t  {{SESSION_DIR}}/<MANIFEST.outputs.primary>\n18\tDo NOT derive output filenames from TASK_FILE or workflow names.\n19\t\n20\tThe structured prompt must follow this contract:\n21\t- Optionally include a `## CONTEXT` section before `## SLOTS` when TASK_FILE contains\n22\t  global objective, background, physical setting, or shared framing that applies\n23\t  to multiple work items.\n24\t- Contain a `## SLOTS` section.\n25\t- Contain a `## CONSTRAINTS` section for true global constraints only.\n26\t- Preserve the original intent and ordering of TASK_FILE.\n27\t- Represent each distinct underlying work item once.\n28\t- Avoid audit notes, coverage notes, citations, confidence footnotes, source\n29\t  IDs, hidden rationale, or implementation details in the generated prompt.\n30\t\n31\tSlot definition:\n32\t- A slot is a distinct underlying work item requested by TASK_FILE.\n33\t- A slot is NOT every mention, bullet, sentence, or paraphrase.\n34\t- Global objective, background, physical setting, and shared context are\n35\t  framing material, not slots, unless TASK_FILE explicitly asks for them as a\n36\t  separate question, deliverable, output, or work item.\n37\t- Repeated or paraphrased requests MUST be merged into one slot.\n38\t- Separate slots are allowed only when TASK_FILE gives a clear discriminant:\n39\t  topic, entity, scale, method, regime, period, or output.\n40\t- Tightly coupled sub-steps of one underlying work item stay in that slot.\n41\t- If two candidate final slots cannot be separated using only TASK_FILE, fail\n42\t  instead of emitting an ambiguous prompt.\n43\t\n44\tMethod:\n45\t\n46\t1) Read TASK_FILE and identify candidate work items\n47\t   - Read TASK_FILE once, end to end.\n48\t   - Identify explicit questions, subtasks, requested outputs, and deliverables.\n49\t   - Identify global objective, background, physical setting, and shared\n50\t     context that frame multiple work items.\n51\t   - Group candidates by underlying work item before writing any slot.\n52\t   - Merge repeated/paraphrased candidates unless TASK_FILE gives a clear\n53\t     discriminant: topic, entity, scale, method, regime, period, or output.\n54\t   - Preserve the order of first appearance for the final merged work items.\n55\t   - Identify explicit global constraints that clearly apply to the entire\n56\t     downstream workflow, not just to one work item.\n57\t   - Do not infer voice, notation, preferred sources, or execution policy from\n58\t     context unless TASK_FILE states them explicitly as document-wide\n59\t     requirements.\n60\t\n61\t2) Build optional CONTEXT section\n62\t   - If TASK_FILE contains global objective, background, physical setting, or\n63\t     shared context that applies to multiple slots, add `## CONTEXT` before\n64\t     `## SLOTS`.\n65\t   - The CONTEXT section must use only TASK_FILE information.\n66\t   - CONTEXT is framing only. Do not include separate work-item instructions\n67\t     there, and do not promote framing material into a slot.\n68\t   - If TASK_FILE contains no useful shared framing, omit `## CONTEXT`.\n69\t\n70\t3) Build SLOTS section\n71\t   - Add the heading `## SLOTS`.\n72\t   - Create one slot per distinct underlying work item after grouping.\n73\t   - For each slot, add a heading:\n74\t     - `### SLOT N: <short title>` where N is 1-based and `<short title>` is\n75\t       derived from TASK_FILE wording.\n76\t   - Under each slot heading, add a short description that:\n77\t     - Uses only information from TASK_FILE.\n78\t     - States what must be done for that work item.\n79\t     - Preserves slot-specific instructions inside the slot.\n80\t     - Does not introduce new objectives or sub-goals.\n81\t     - Does not repeat global constraints that belong in `## CONSTRAINTS`.\n82\t     - Ends with presentation guidance only when TASK_FILE gives a format\n83\t       signal. If TASK_FILE gives no format signal, end with:\n84\t       \"Write in flowing paragraphs.\"\n85\t\n86\t4) Build CONSTRAINTS section\n87\t   - Add the heading `## CONSTRAINTS`.\n88\t   - List only explicit document-wide constraints as `- ...` bullets, such as:\n89\t     - Language or locale requirements for the entire output.\n90\t     - Document-wide structural rules explicitly stated in TASK_FILE.\n91\t     - Global length/formatting constraints that apply to every slot.\n92\t   - Do not move slot-specific instructions into global CONSTRAINTS.\n93\t   - Constraints MUST be paraphrases of explicit, document-wide requirements\n94\t     stated in TASK_FILE.\n95\t   - Do not invent constraints.\n96\t   - Do not describe tools, workflows, executor behavior, or implementation\n97\t     details here.\n98\t\n99\t5) Internal coverage and overlap check\n100\t   - Use TASK_FILE as the only evidence source.\n101\t   - Use AUDIT_RULES only as internal discipline. Do not emit AUDIT_RULES\n102\t     formatting or audit-style notes in the generated prompt.\n103\t   - Verify that each final slot is justified by TASK_FILE.\n104\t   - Verify that every explicit underlying work item is covered once.\n105\t   - Verify that global objective/background/context is not emitted as a slot\n106\t     unless TASK_FILE explicitly requests it as a separate work item.\n107\t   - Verify that global constraints are not repeated in slots.\n108\t   - Compare final slots pairwise:\n109\t     - Merge duplicates and paraphrases.\n110\t     - Keep separate slots only when their discriminant is explicit in TASK_FILE:\n111\t       topic, entity, scale, method, regime, period, or output.\n112\t     - If separation is still ambiguous, stop and report the conflict instead\n113\t       of writing the prompt.\n114\t\n115\t6) Apply formatting rules and write output file\n116\t   - If FORMAT_RULES is a non-empty path, read and apply those style rules\n117\t     without changing slot meaning or constraint meaning.\n118\t   - Read MANIFEST and use `outputs.primary` as the only valid output filename.\n119\t   - Use Write tool to save the structured prompt to:\n120\t     {{SESSION_DIR}}/<MANIFEST.outputs.primary>\n121\t   - Write only the prompt document with optional `## CONTEXT`, required\n122\t     `## SLOTS`, and required `## CONSTRAINTS`.\n123\t   - Confirm: \"Structured prompt written to {{SESSION_DIR}}/<MANIFEST.outputs.primary>\"\n124\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"478ab821-ce71-4d9a-9c86-7b54770e6fcb","uuid":"559b40f5-08ad-44d9-948a-0c12cd823d57","timestamp":"2026-07-06T17:09:00.784Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/usr/local/libexec/tito/workflows/prompt.session.md","content":"PROMPT WORKFLOW (TASK -> SLOT-BASED STRUCTURED PROMPT)\n\nYou will read a free-form TASK_FILE and produce one normalized prompt document\nwith optional CONTEXT plus a fixed SLOTS + CONSTRAINTS layout.\n\nInputs (absolute paths provided):\n- TASK_FILE: {{TASK_FILE}}\n- INPUT_DIR (optional): {{INPUT_DIR}}         # Not used for evidence in this workflow\n- SESSION_DIR: {{SESSION_DIR}}\n- FORMAT_RULES: {{FORMAT_RULES_PATH}}        # rules/format.session.md (style only)\n- AUDIT_RULES: {{AUDIT_RULES_PATH}}          # Internal check discipline only\n- MANIFEST: {{SESSION_DIR}}/manifest.json    # CLI-provided manifest (authoritative I/O)\n\nGoal:\nProduce ONE markdown file representing the structured prompt.\nOutput filename is controlled by MANIFEST:\n  {{SESSION_DIR}}/<MANIFEST.outputs.primary>\nDo NOT derive output filenames from TASK_FILE or workflow names.\n\nThe structured prompt must follow this contract:\n- Optionally include a `## CONTEXT` section before `## SLOTS` when TASK_FILE contains\n  global objective, background, physical setting, or shared framing that applies\n  to multiple work items.\n- Contain a `## SLOTS` section.\n- Contain a `## CONSTRAINTS` section for true global constraints only.\n- Preserve the original intent and ordering of TASK_FILE.\n- Represent each distinct underlying work item once.\n- Avoid audit notes, coverage notes, citations, confidence footnotes, source\n  IDs, hidden rationale, or implementation details in the generated prompt.\n\nSlot definition:\n- A slot is a distinct underlying work item requested by TASK_FILE.\n- A slot is NOT every mention, bullet, sentence, or paraphrase.\n- Global objective, background, physical setting, and shared context are\n  framing material, not slots, unless TASK_FILE explicitly asks for them as a\n  separate question, deliverable, output, or work item.\n- Repeated or paraphrased requests MUST be merged into one slot.\n- Separate slots are allowed only when TASK_FILE gives a clear discriminant:\n  topic, entity, scale, method, regime, period, or output.\n- Tightly coupled sub-steps of one underlying work item stay in that slot.\n- If two candidate final slots cannot be separated using only TASK_FILE, fail\n  instead of emitting an ambiguous prompt.\n\nMethod:\n\n1) Read TASK_FILE and identify candidate work items\n   - Read TASK_FILE once, end to end.\n   - Identify explicit questions, subtasks, requested outputs, and deliverables.\n   - Identify global objective, background, physical setting, and shared\n     context that frame multiple work items.\n   - Group candidates by underlying work item before writing any slot.\n   - Merge repeated/paraphrased candidates unless TASK_FILE gives a clear\n     discriminant: topic, entity, scale, method, regime, period, or output.\n   - Preserve the order of first appearance for the final merged work items.\n   - Identify explicit global constraints that clearly apply to the entire\n     downstream workflow, not just to one work item.\n   - Do not infer voice, notation, preferred sources, or execution policy from\n     context unless TASK_FILE states them explicitly as document-wide\n     requirements.\n\n2) Build optional CONTEXT section\n   - If TASK_FILE contains global objective, background, physical setting, or\n     shared context that applies to multiple slots, add `## CONTEXT` before\n     `## SLOTS`.\n   - The CONTEXT section must use only TASK_FILE information.\n   - CONTEXT is framing only. Do not include separate work-item instructions\n     there, and do not promote framing material into a slot.\n   - If TASK_FILE contains no useful shared framing, omit `## CONTEXT`.\n\n3) Build SLOTS section\n   - Add the heading `## SLOTS`.\n   - Create one slot per distinct underlying work item after grouping.\n   - For each slot, add a heading:\n     - `### SLOT N: <short title>` where N is 1-based and `<short title>` is\n       derived from TASK_FILE wording.\n   - Under each slot heading, add a short description that:\n     - Uses only information from TASK_FILE.\n     - States what must be done for that work item.\n     - Preserves slot-specific instructions inside the slot.\n     - Does not introduce new objectives or sub-goals.\n     - Does not repeat global constraints that belong in `## CONSTRAINTS`.\n     - Ends with presentation guidance only when TASK_FILE gives a format\n       signal. If TASK_FILE gives no format signal, end with:\n       \"Write in flowing paragraphs.\"\n\n4) Build CONSTRAINTS section\n   - Add the heading `## CONSTRAINTS`.\n   - List only explicit document-wide constraints as `- ...` bullets, such as:\n     - Language or locale requirements for the entire output.\n     - Document-wide structural rules explicitly stated in TASK_FILE.\n     - Global length/formatting constraints that apply to every slot.\n   - Do not move slot-specific instructions into global CONSTRAINTS.\n   - Constraints MUST be paraphrases of explicit, document-wide requirements\n     stated in TASK_FILE.\n   - Do not invent constraints.\n   - Do not describe tools, workflows, executor behavior, or implementation\n     details here.\n\n5) Internal coverage and overlap check\n   - Use TASK_FILE as the only evidence source.\n   - Use AUDIT_RULES only as internal discipline. Do not emit AUDIT_RULES\n     formatting or audit-style notes in the generated prompt.\n   - Verify that each final slot is justified by TASK_FILE.\n   - Verify that every explicit underlying work item is covered once.\n   - Verify that global objective/background/context is not emitted as a slot\n     unless TASK_FILE explicitly requests it as a separate work item.\n   - Verify that global constraints are not repeated in slots.\n   - Compare final slots pairwise:\n     - Merge duplicates and paraphrases.\n     - Keep separate slots only when their discriminant is explicit in TASK_FILE:\n       topic, entity, scale, method, regime, period, or output.\n     - If separation is still ambiguous, stop and report the conflict instead\n       of writing the prompt.\n\n6) Apply formatting rules and write output file\n   - If FORMAT_RULES is a non-empty path, read and apply those style rules\n     without changing slot meaning or constraint meaning.\n   - Read MANIFEST and use `outputs.primary` as the only valid output filename.\n   - Use Write tool to save the structured prompt to:\n     {{SESSION_DIR}}/<MANIFEST.outputs.primary>\n   - Write only the prompt document with optional `## CONTEXT`, required\n     `## SLOTS`, and required `## CONSTRAINTS`.\n   - Confirm: \"Structured prompt written to {{SESSION_DIR}}/<MANIFEST.outputs.primary>\"\n","numLines":124,"startLine":1,"totalLines":124}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01T9Qt3SwDEZzFrVDhgVNhiB","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Qg77ssP8mAxzKi7rnzJTQm","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/usr/local/libexec/tito/rules/format.session.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":35767,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":35767,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"478ab821-ce71-4d9a-9c86-7b54770e6fcb","uuid":"99b2931c-2fca-4d28-84ee-a2324058bbae","request_id":"req_011CcmAyS76WeGEfrf7BGjQB"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Qg77ssP8mAxzKi7rnzJTQm","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tPROFESSIONAL DOCUMENT STYLE FORMATTING\n2\t\n3\tPurpose: Transform technical documents into publication-ready professional format.\n4\t\n5\tIMPORTANT: BEFORE FORMATTING - ANALYZE DOCUMENT STATE\n6\t\n7\tPRE-FORMAT ANALYSIS REQUIREMENTS:\n8\t1. **Check Current Format**: Assess if the document already meets professional clarity and consistency\n9\t2. **Mathematical Notation Check**: Verify if equations use proper LaTeX format ($...$, $$...$$) where applicable\n10\t3. **Citation Format**: Ensure citations follow project token policy or normalized [@key]\n11\t4. **Quality Assessment**: Determine if the document exhibits publication-ready professional characteristics\n12\t5. **De-duplication and Consistency**: Detect duplicated content and potential contradictions; resolve when possible\n13\t\n14\tQUALITY TARGETS (NON-GATING):\n15\t- Clear organization suitable for a professional technical audience\n16\t- Mathematical expressions (if present) in LaTeX format ($...$ or `$$...$$`)\n17\t- Consistent, normalized citations (project tokens or [@key])\n18\t- Professional/academic voice and consistent technical terminology\n19\t\n20\tNote: These targets are mandatory goals to achieve. Always apply all formatting checks regardless of perceived document quality.\n21\t\n22\tMATHEMATICAL EXPRESSIONS:\n23\t\n24\tInline Math ($...$ format):\n25\t- Use for mathematical symbols, variables, and simple expressions within text\n26\t- Examples: $x$, $f(x)$, $n > 100$, $\\alpha = 0.05$, $O(n \\log n)$\n27\t- Use for brief formulas: $E[X] = \\mu$, $\\beta_1 = \\mu_3^2/\\mu_2^3$\n28\t- Single-line expressions that flow with text must be short: $Z = \\gamma + \\delta f((X - \\xi)/\\lambda)$\n29\t\n30\tDisplay Math ($$...$$ format):\n31\t- Use for standalone equations that deserve emphasis or are complex\n32\t- Multi-line expressions, definitions, theorems\n33\t- Integration/summation with multiple terms: $$H[f] = -\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} f(x) \\log f(x) dx$$\n34\t- Matrix expressions, optimization problems\n35\t- Equations with fractions, large operators, or multiple levels: $$\\kappa = \\frac{\\beta_1(\\beta_2+3)^2}{4(4\\beta_2-3\\beta_1)(2\\beta_2-3\\beta_1-6)}$$\n36\t- Piecewise functions, case definitions\n37\t\n38\tDisplay Math Promotion Gate:\n39\t- Before final save, scan every inline `$...$` math span.\n40\t- Convert an inline span to a standalone `$$...$$` display block when the\n41\t  span is a complete equation or definition and contains any of these\n42\t  display triggers: `\\int`, `\\sum`, `\\prod`, `\\lim`, `\\max`, `\\min`,\n43\t  `\\frac{`, `\\sqrt{`, `\\left`, `\\right`, `\\begin{`, `cases`, `matrix`,\n44\t  `aligned`.\n45\t- Convert named engineering index or ratio definitions to display math when\n46\t  the equation is the object being defined, for example peak/RMS/effective\n47\t  strain measures, CSR, MSF, LPI, LSN, FOS/FS, PGA-derived demand, or\n48\t  pore-pressure ratio definitions.\n49\t- Keep inline math for short symbols, units, variable references, and compact\n50\t  relations that are incidental to the sentence.\n51\t- Do not duplicate the equation: promote by moving the equation to display\n52\t  form and keep the surrounding prose as prose.\n53\t\n54\tFORBIDDEN Math Formats:\n55\t- Never use \\(...\\) or \\[...\\]\n56\t- No Unicode symbols in equations (β, α, ∑, ∫)\n57\t- No inconsistent notation (use $\\lambda$ throughout, not switching to $l$)\n58\t- No equations in code blocks unless showing actual code\n59\t\n60\tMath Notation Standards:\n61\t- Greek letters: $\\alpha, \\beta, \\gamma, \\delta, \\lambda, \\mu, \\sigma, \\theta, \\phi, \\psi, \\omega$\n62\t- Operators: $\\sum, \\prod, \\int, \\partial, \\nabla, \\infty$\n63\t- Relations: $\\leq, \\geq, \\neq, \\approx, \\equiv, \\in, \\subset$\n64\t- Functions: $\\log, \\ln, \\sin, \\cos, \\exp, \\sqrt{}, \\frac{}{}$\n65\t- Define all symbols clearly on first use\n66\t\n67\tPROSE STYLE:\n68\t\n69\tProfessional/Academic Voice:\n70\t- Impersonal, objective tone\n71\t- Present tense for established facts, past tense for specific studies\n72\t- Avoid promotional/commercial language\n73\t- Remove instructional tone\n74\t- Convert to neutral academic phrasing\n75\t\n76\tProhibited rhetorical modes (applies to all workflows)\n77\t- Instructional language: no imperatives, reader directions, or authorial roadmaps (e.g., addressing what the reader should do or what the text \"will\" do next)\n78\t- Motivational/promotional claims: no value‑laden benefits to an audience, no capability or marketing assertions about the work; avoid audience‑targeted promises\n79\t- Second‑person address: do not address the reader directly\n80\t- Rewrite such expressions as descriptive, evidence‑based statements in an impersonal, declarative form\n81\t\n82\tProblematic Language Patterns (convert to academic tone):\n83\t- \"enables\" -> \"permits\", \"allows\", \"makes possible\"\n84\t- \"powerful\"-> \"effective\", \"capable\"\n85\t- \"cutting-edge\" → \"recent\", \"current\", \"modern\"\n86\t\n87\tCHARACTER FORMATTING:\n88\t- For ENGLISH outputs: ASCII text only (no accented letters or non-ASCII punctuation)\n89\t- For non-English outputs: allow the minimal extended Latin characters required by the target language (e.g., á, é, ñ) but keep punctuation ASCII\n90\t- Straight quotes (\", ') not smart quotes (\", \", ', ')\n91\t- Standard hyphens (-) not em-dashes (—)\n92\t- No special Unicode symbols outside LaTeX math\n93\t\n94\tDE-DUPLICATION AND CONSISTENCY CHECKS:\n95\t- Identify and remove duplicated sentences, sections, tables, or figures.\n96\t- Detect contradictory statements; reconcile using KB and external citations; if unresolved, keep the more reliable/cited version.\n97\t- Ensure units, symbols, and terminology are consistent throughout.\n98\t\n99\tCITATIONS — Session workflow policy (RESEARCH/REVIEW)\n100\t\n101\tMANDATORY REQUIREMENT:\n102\t- PRESERVE all token citations in the final document exactly as they appear\n103\t- Supported tokens: [KB:filename.md], [WEB:https://...], [DOI:...], [ARXIV:...]\n104\t- Do NOT convert tokens to [@key] format\n105\t- Do NOT remove any citation tokens\n106\t- Reasoning: Token citations are required for downstream audit verification and traceability\n107\t\n108\tDeduplication:\n109\t- Ensure a single consistent citation per claim\n110\t- If multiple tokens cite the same source, keep one representative token\n111\t- Place citations immediately after the sentence they support\n112\t\n113\tCODE BLOCKS (MARKDOWN/PANDOC/QUARTO)\n114\t- Treat fenced code blocks (backticks or tildes; length >= 3, including 4+; may include Quarto language headers like `{r}` on the opening fence) and indented code blocks as **opaque**.\n115\t- Do NOT modify content inside code blocks during formatting.\n116\t- Do NOT insert citations or audit footnotes inside code blocks; place any required metadata outside the block (typically immediately after the closing fence).\n117\t\n118\tFILESYSTEM AND LOG ACCESS CONSTRAINTS:\n119\t- NEVER read or use orchestration log files as input to any workflow. In particular, you MUST NOT read files such as `agent.out`, `RUN_METADATA.txt`, or other raw transcripts produced by the execution layer; they exist only for debugging on disk and must not be sent back into Claude-flow or used as evidence.\n120\t- When using filesystem tools (Read/Glob/Bash/find), skip any entries named exactly `current` or obvious symlinks to `*/sessions/run-*`; treat them as internal implementation details, not as documents or data sources.\n121\t\n122\tOUTPUT REQUIREMENT:\n123\tProduce a publication-ready professional document requiring no additional formatting.\n124\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"478ab821-ce71-4d9a-9c86-7b54770e6fcb","uuid":"5e1c8d12-8c93-4b3b-8b28-3cc53462154e","timestamp":"2026-07-06T17:09:01.255Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/usr/local/libexec/tito/rules/format.session.md","content":"PROFESSIONAL DOCUMENT STYLE FORMATTING\n\nPurpose: Transform technical documents into publication-ready professional format.\n\nIMPORTANT: BEFORE FORMATTING - ANALYZE DOCUMENT STATE\n\nPRE-FORMAT ANALYSIS REQUIREMENTS:\n1. **Check Current Format**: Assess if the document already meets professional clarity and consistency\n2. **Mathematical Notation Check**: Verify if equations use proper LaTeX format ($...$, $$...$$) where applicable\n3. **Citation Format**: Ensure citations follow project token policy or normalized [@key]\n4. **Quality Assessment**: Determine if the document exhibits publication-ready professional characteristics\n5. **De-duplication and Consistency**: Detect duplicated content and potential contradictions; resolve when possible\n\nQUALITY TARGETS (NON-GATING):\n- Clear organization suitable for a professional technical audience\n- Mathematical expressions (if present) in LaTeX format ($...$ or `$$...$$`)\n- Consistent, normalized citations (project tokens or [@key])\n- Professional/academic voice and consistent technical terminology\n\nNote: These targets are mandatory goals to achieve. Always apply all formatting checks regardless of perceived document quality.\n\nMATHEMATICAL EXPRESSIONS:\n\nInline Math ($...$ format):\n- Use for mathematical symbols, variables, and simple expressions within text\n- Examples: $x$, $f(x)$, $n > 100$, $\\alpha = 0.05$, $O(n \\log n)$\n- Use for brief formulas: $E[X] = \\mu$, $\\beta_1 = \\mu_3^2/\\mu_2^3$\n- Single-line expressions that flow with text must be short: $Z = \\gamma + \\delta f((X - \\xi)/\\lambda)$\n\nDisplay Math ($$...$$ format):\n- Use for standalone equations that deserve emphasis or are complex\n- Multi-line expressions, definitions, theorems\n- Integration/summation with multiple terms: $$H[f] = -\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} f(x) \\log f(x) dx$$\n- Matrix expressions, optimization problems\n- Equations with fractions, large operators, or multiple levels: $$\\kappa = \\frac{\\beta_1(\\beta_2+3)^2}{4(4\\beta_2-3\\beta_1)(2\\beta_2-3\\beta_1-6)}$$\n- Piecewise functions, case definitions\n\nDisplay Math Promotion Gate:\n- Before final save, scan every inline `$...$` math span.\n- Convert an inline span to a standalone `$$...$$` display block when the\n  span is a complete equation or definition and contains any of these\n  display triggers: `\\int`, `\\sum`, `\\prod`, `\\lim`, `\\max`, `\\min`,\n  `\\frac{`, `\\sqrt{`, `\\left`, `\\right`, `\\begin{`, `cases`, `matrix`,\n  `aligned`.\n- Convert named engineering index or ratio definitions to display math when\n  the equation is the object being defined, for example peak/RMS/effective\n  strain measures, CSR, MSF, LPI, LSN, FOS/FS, PGA-derived demand, or\n  pore-pressure ratio definitions.\n- Keep inline math for short symbols, units, variable references, and compact\n  relations that are incidental to the sentence.\n- Do not duplicate the equation: promote by moving the equation to display\n  form and keep the surrounding prose as prose.\n\nFORBIDDEN Math Formats:\n- Never use \\(...\\) or \\[...\\]\n- No Unicode symbols in equations (β, α, ∑, ∫)\n- No inconsistent notation (use $\\lambda$ throughout, not switching to $l$)\n- No equations in code blocks unless showing actual code\n\nMath Notation Standards:\n- Greek letters: $\\alpha, \\beta, \\gamma, \\delta, \\lambda, \\mu, \\sigma, \\theta, \\phi, \\psi, \\omega$\n- Operators: $\\sum, \\prod, \\int, \\partial, \\nabla, \\infty$\n- Relations: $\\leq, \\geq, \\neq, \\approx, \\equiv, \\in, \\subset$\n- Functions: $\\log, \\ln, \\sin, \\cos, \\exp, \\sqrt{}, \\frac{}{}$\n- Define all symbols clearly on first use\n\nPROSE STYLE:\n\nProfessional/Academic Voice:\n- Impersonal, objective tone\n- Present tense for established facts, past tense for specific studies\n- Avoid promotional/commercial language\n- Remove instructional tone\n- Convert to neutral academic phrasing\n\nProhibited rhetorical modes (applies to all workflows)\n- Instructional language: no imperatives, reader directions, or authorial roadmaps (e.g., addressing what the reader should do or what the text \"will\" do next)\n- Motivational/promotional claims: no value‑laden benefits to an audience, no capability or marketing assertions about the work; avoid audience‑targeted promises\n- Second‑person address: do not address the reader directly\n- Rewrite such expressions as descriptive, evidence‑based statements in an impersonal, declarative form\n\nProblematic Language Patterns (convert to academic tone):\n- \"enables\" -> \"permits\", \"allows\", \"makes possible\"\n- \"powerful\"-> \"effective\", \"capable\"\n- \"cutting-edge\" → \"recent\", \"current\", \"modern\"\n\nCHARACTER FORMATTING:\n- For ENGLISH outputs: ASCII text only (no accented letters or non-ASCII punctuation)\n- For non-English outputs: allow the minimal extended Latin characters required by the target language (e.g., á, é, ñ) but keep punctuation ASCII\n- Straight quotes (\", ') not smart quotes (\", \", ', ')\n- Standard hyphens (-) not em-dashes (—)\n- No special Unicode symbols outside LaTeX math\n\nDE-DUPLICATION AND CONSISTENCY CHECKS:\n- Identify and remove duplicated sentences, sections, tables, or figures.\n- Detect contradictory statements; reconcile using KB and external citations; if unresolved, keep the more reliable/cited version.\n- Ensure units, symbols, and terminology are consistent throughout.\n\nCITATIONS — Session workflow policy (RESEARCH/REVIEW)\n\nMANDATORY REQUIREMENT:\n- PRESERVE all token citations in the final document exactly as they appear\n- Supported tokens: [KB:filename.md], [WEB:https://...], [DOI:...], [ARXIV:...]\n- Do NOT convert tokens to [@key] format\n- Do NOT remove any citation tokens\n- Reasoning: Token citations are required for downstream audit verification and traceability\n\nDeduplication:\n- Ensure a single consistent citation per claim\n- If multiple tokens cite the same source, keep one representative token\n- Place citations immediately after the sentence they support\n\nCODE BLOCKS (MARKDOWN/PANDOC/QUARTO)\n- Treat fenced code blocks (backticks or tildes; length >= 3, including 4+; may include Quarto language headers like `{r}` on the opening fence) and indented code blocks as **opaque**.\n- Do NOT modify content inside code blocks during formatting.\n- Do NOT insert citations or audit footnotes inside code blocks; place any required metadata outside the block (typically immediately after the closing fence).\n\nFILESYSTEM AND LOG ACCESS CONSTRAINTS:\n- NEVER read or use orchestration log files as input to any workflow. In particular, you MUST NOT read files such as `agent.out`, `RUN_METADATA.txt`, or other raw transcripts produced by the execution layer; they exist only for debugging on disk and must not be sent back into Claude-flow or used as evidence.\n- When using filesystem tools (Read/Glob/Bash/find), skip any entries named exactly `current` or obvious symlinks to `*/sessions/run-*`; treat them as internal implementation details, not as documents or data sources.\n\nOUTPUT REQUIREMENT:\nProduce a publication-ready professional document requiring no additional formatting.\n","numLines":124,"startLine":1,"totalLines":124}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01T9Qt3SwDEZzFrVDhgVNhiB","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01CMWsFDUaTprqMHaiudS4SR","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/usr/local/libexec/tito/rules/audit.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":35767,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":35767,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"478ab821-ce71-4d9a-9c86-7b54770e6fcb","uuid":"774d0a13-761d-499e-a785-506d6506a72e","request_id":"req_011CcmAyS76WeGEfrf7BGjQB"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CMWsFDUaTprqMHaiudS4SR","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# AUDIT RULES — Paragraph-level veracity verification (CORE)\n2\t\n3\tPurpose: Define the universal structure and taxonomy for audit footnotes across all workflows that require veracity checking.\n4\t\n5\t## Scope\n6\tThis rule file contains workflow-agnostic elements:\n7\t- Footnote format specification\n8\t- Hallucination taxonomy\n9\t- Confidence level definitions\n10\t- Enforcement policy (no pass-through)\n11\t\n12\tEvidence policies (target scope, fetch policy, token requirements) are workflow-specific and declared inline in each workflow file.\n13\t\n14\t## Enforcement policy\n15\t- No pass-through: Always perform the per-paragraph footnote audit; never skip based on perceived document quality.\n16\t- Audit timing: Perform audit AFTER draft assembly and formatting, as final post-processing step.\n17\t- Apply audit to every paragraph in the output document.\n18\t\n19\t**PARAGRAPH DEFINITION** (for audit purposes):\n20\tA **paragraph** is a block of text separated by blank lines in Markdown. Each blank-line-separated block requires its own audit footnote, regardless of:\n21\t- Section headers (##, ###) - sections may contain multiple paragraphs\n22\t- Conceptual grouping - each text block is audited independently\n23\t- List items - each numbered/bulleted item that forms a paragraph needs audit\n24\t\n25\tCount paragraphs by counting text blocks separated by blank lines, NOT by counting sections or concepts.\n26\t\n27\t**CODE BLOCKS (fenced or indented) are NOT paragraphs for audit purposes.**\n28\t- Treat any code block as an **opaque, atomic block**.\n29\t- You MUST NOT insert audit footnotes inside a code block.\n30\t- If a workflow requires an audit judgment about a code block (for example, to flag that code is likely incorrect), place a single audit footnote **immediately after the closing fence** (or after the indented block ends) on the next line.\n31\t- A code block may contain blank lines; these must not be treated as paragraph boundaries.\n32\t\n33\t**DISPLAY MATH BLOCKS (for example standalone `$$...$$`, `\\[...\\]`, or equation environments) are NOT paragraphs for audit purposes.**\n34\t- Treat a standalone display-math block as a non-prose block, analogous to a code block.\n35\t- Do NOT require or insert an audit footnote on the math block itself.\n36\t- Any prose paragraph that introduces, interprets, or follows the equation is still a paragraph and MUST keep its own audit footnote.\n37\t- Do NOT assume that a footnote after a display equation covers an earlier prose paragraph separated by a blank line.\n38\t\n39\t## Footnote format (mandatory, exact)\n40\tFor each paragraph, append exactly one audit footnote at the end in this exact format:\n41\t\n42\t```\n43\t^[Confidence: HIGH|MEDIUM|LOW, Rationale: explanatory note]\n44\t```\n45\t\n46\tRequirements:\n47\t- Place at paragraph end (after all sentences and inline citations).\n48\t- Do NOT place audit metadata (`Confidence:`, `Rationale:`) inline in prose; use footnotes only.\n49\t- Must include both components: `Confidence` and `Rationale`.\n50\t- Rationale must be specific and evidence-grounded. By default it should give a detailed explanation referencing concrete evidence and issues found, but individual workflows may further constrain length or style (for example, short 1–2 sentence rationales) as long as they still explain why the confidence level and rationale are appropriate.\n51\t\n52\t## Interpreting audit judgments (Rationale replaces structured Verdict)\n53\t\n54\tThis specification no longer defines a structured `Verdict` field. All judgments about whether a paragraph is supported, unsupported, or uncertain MUST be expressed in natural language inside the `Rationale`, using the workflow's own evidence policy.\n55\t\n56\tGuidance:\n57\t- When the available evidence **supports** the paragraph according to the workflow's evidence policy, the rationale should say this explicitly (for example, that the claims are well supported and consistent with the cited sources).\n58\t- When the evidence **contradicts** the paragraph or reveals clear factual errors, the rationale should state that the paragraph is likely incorrect and explain why.\n59\t- When evidence is **insufficient, ambiguous, or mixed**, the rationale should explain the uncertainty and, where relevant, which parts are supported and which are not.\n60\t\n61\tHistorical note:\n62\t- Older outputs may include a structured prefix like `Verdict: TRUE|FALSE|UNCERTAIN` inside the footnote. This text is now considered diagnostic only. New workflows MUST NOT rely on it for selection or filtering, and MUST NOT introduce a `Verdict:` field as part of the contract.\n63\t\n64\t## Confidence levels\n65\t\n66\t### HIGH\n67\t- The technical claim is correct and well supported by authoritative evidence\n68\t- Multiple credible sources confirm the substance of the statement\n69\t- No contradictions detected in the technical content\n70\t- Confidence measures whether the CONTENT is correct, not whether\n71\t  bibliographic metadata is perfect. A correctly identified source with\n72\t  a minor year discrepancy, an ambiguous edition, or inaccessible full\n73\t  text does not reduce confidence when the technical claim itself is\n74\t  verified. Report bibliographic observations in Rationale.\n75\t\n76\t### MEDIUM\n77\t- The technical content is plausible but evidence is incomplete or mixed\n78\t- The claim is partially supported: some aspects are confirmed while\n79\t  others lack independent verification\n80\t- The methodology or formulation described may have known limitations\n81\t  that the paragraph does not acknowledge\n82\t- Evidence exists but from a single source without independent\n83\t  corroboration of the specific technical claim\n84\t\n85\t### LOW\n86\t- The technical content lacks credible supporting evidence\n87\t- Available evidence contradicts the claim or reveals factual errors\n88\t- The claim relies on a single questionable source or no source at all\n89\t- Significant methodological errors or inconsistencies in substance\n90\t- The paragraph makes quantitative claims without any citation support\n91\t\n92\t## Hallucination taxonomy (apply via rationale)\n93\t\n94\tWhen assessing paragraphs, check for these common hallucination types and reflect findings in the rationale (and, implicitly, in the chosen confidence level):\n95\t\n96\t1. **Unsupported quantitative claims**: Specific numbers, percentages, measurements, or statistics without citation support.\n97\t\n98\t2. **Internal contradictions**: Paragraph contradicts itself or contradicts other paragraphs in the document.\n99\t\n100\t3. **Equations inconsistent with cited sources**: Mathematical expressions, formulas, or models that do not match the notation, parameters, or structure in cited sources.\n101\t\n102\t4. **Terminology misuse**: Technical terms, definitions, or classifications used incorrectly relative to cited definitions or domain-standard meanings.\n103\t\n104\t5. **Chronological inconsistencies**: Dates, timelines, or temporal sequences that conflict with cited sources or known facts within the document.\n105\t\n106\t## Workflow-specific overrides\n107\t\n108\tEach workflow that uses this rule MUST specify inline, in its own workflow file:\n109\t- **Target scope**: What is being audited (generated output vs. input documents under review).\n110\t- **Evidence rule**: What evidence sources are acceptable for audit judgments.\n111\t- **Fetch policy**: Whether new source fetching is allowed/required during audit.\n112\t- **Token requirements**: Which citation token types are expected (`[KB:/WEB:/DOI:/ARXIV:/ZOTERO:]`).\n113\t- **Audit semantics**: How `Confidence` and `Rationale` should be interpreted for that workflow (for example, veracity vs external sources, translation fidelity vs source text, coverage vs KB, etc.).\n114\t\n115\tFor current workflows, see in particular: `workflows/research.session.md`, `workflows/review.session.md`, `workflows/enhance.session.md`, and `workflows/translate.session.md`. These specializations MUST remain consistent with the base semantics defined in this file.\n116\t\n117\tAdditional design or analysis documents may exist in `docs/`, but the only normative contract for audit footnote structure and taxonomy is this file.\n118\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"478ab821-ce71-4d9a-9c86-7b54770e6fcb","uuid":"25605214-3eac-4ab1-8406-a1dc0a198dc7","timestamp":"2026-07-06T17:09:01.684Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/usr/local/libexec/tito/rules/audit.md","content":"# AUDIT RULES — Paragraph-level veracity verification (CORE)\n\nPurpose: Define the universal structure and taxonomy for audit footnotes across all workflows that require veracity checking.\n\n## Scope\nThis rule file contains workflow-agnostic elements:\n- Footnote format specification\n- Hallucination taxonomy\n- Confidence level definitions\n- Enforcement policy (no pass-through)\n\nEvidence policies (target scope, fetch policy, token requirements) are workflow-specific and declared inline in each workflow file.\n\n## Enforcement policy\n- No pass-through: Always perform the per-paragraph footnote audit; never skip based on perceived document quality.\n- Audit timing: Perform audit AFTER draft assembly and formatting, as final post-processing step.\n- Apply audit to every paragraph in the output document.\n\n**PARAGRAPH DEFINITION** (for audit purposes):\nA **paragraph** is a block of text separated by blank lines in Markdown. Each blank-line-separated block requires its own audit footnote, regardless of:\n- Section headers (##, ###) - sections may contain multiple paragraphs\n- Conceptual grouping - each text block is audited independently\n- List items - each numbered/bulleted item that forms a paragraph needs audit\n\nCount paragraphs by counting text blocks separated by blank lines, NOT by counting sections or concepts.\n\n**CODE BLOCKS (fenced or indented) are NOT paragraphs for audit purposes.**\n- Treat any code block as an **opaque, atomic block**.\n- You MUST NOT insert audit footnotes inside a code block.\n- If a workflow requires an audit judgment about a code block (for example, to flag that code is likely incorrect), place a single audit footnote **immediately after the closing fence** (or after the indented block ends) on the next line.\n- A code block may contain blank lines; these must not be treated as paragraph boundaries.\n\n**DISPLAY MATH BLOCKS (for example standalone `$$...$$`, `\\[...\\]`, or equation environments) are NOT paragraphs for audit purposes.**\n- Treat a standalone display-math block as a non-prose block, analogous to a code block.\n- Do NOT require or insert an audit footnote on the math block itself.\n- Any prose paragraph that introduces, interprets, or follows the equation is still a paragraph and MUST keep its own audit footnote.\n- Do NOT assume that a footnote after a display equation covers an earlier prose paragraph separated by a blank line.\n\n## Footnote format (mandatory, exact)\nFor each paragraph, append exactly one audit footnote at the end in this exact format:\n\n```\n^[Confidence: HIGH|MEDIUM|LOW, Rationale: explanatory note]\n```\n\nRequirements:\n- Place at paragraph end (after all sentences and inline citations).\n- Do NOT place audit metadata (`Confidence:`, `Rationale:`) inline in prose; use footnotes only.\n- Must include both components: `Confidence` and `Rationale`.\n- Rationale must be specific and evidence-grounded. By default it should give a detailed explanation referencing concrete evidence and issues found, but individual workflows may further constrain length or style (for example, short 1–2 sentence rationales) as long as they still explain why the confidence level and rationale are appropriate.\n\n## Interpreting audit judgments (Rationale replaces structured Verdict)\n\nThis specification no longer defines a structured `Verdict` field. All judgments about whether a paragraph is supported, unsupported, or uncertain MUST be expressed in natural language inside the `Rationale`, using the workflow's own evidence policy.\n\nGuidance:\n- When the available evidence **supports** the paragraph according to the workflow's evidence policy, the rationale should say this explicitly (for example, that the claims are well supported and consistent with the cited sources).\n- When the evidence **contradicts** the paragraph or reveals clear factual errors, the rationale should state that the paragraph is likely incorrect and explain why.\n- When evidence is **insufficient, ambiguous, or mixed**, the rationale should explain the uncertainty and, where relevant, which parts are supported and which are not.\n\nHistorical note:\n- Older outputs may include a structured prefix like `Verdict: TRUE|FALSE|UNCERTAIN` inside the footnote. This text is now considered diagnostic only. New workflows MUST NOT rely on it for selection or filtering, and MUST NOT introduce a `Verdict:` field as part of the contract.\n\n## Confidence levels\n\n### HIGH\n- The technical claim is correct and well supported by authoritative evidence\n- Multiple credible sources confirm the substance of the statement\n- No contradictions detected in the technical content\n- Confidence measures whether the CONTENT is correct, not whether\n  bibliographic metadata is perfect. A correctly identified source with\n  a minor year discrepancy, an ambiguous edition, or inaccessible full\n  text does not reduce confidence when the technical claim itself is\n  verified. Report bibliographic observations in Rationale.\n\n### MEDIUM\n- The technical content is plausible but evidence is incomplete or mixed\n- The claim is partially supported: some aspects are confirmed while\n  others lack independent verification\n- The methodology or formulation described may have known limitations\n  that the paragraph does not acknowledge\n- Evidence exists but from a single source without independent\n  corroboration of the specific technical claim\n\n### LOW\n- The technical content lacks credible supporting evidence\n- Available evidence contradicts the claim or reveals factual errors\n- The claim relies on a single questionable source or no source at all\n- Significant methodological errors or inconsistencies in substance\n- The paragraph makes quantitative claims without any citation support\n\n## Hallucination taxonomy (apply via rationale)\n\nWhen assessing paragraphs, check for these common hallucination types and reflect findings in the rationale (and, implicitly, in the chosen confidence level):\n\n1. **Unsupported quantitative claims**: Specific numbers, percentages, measurements, or statistics without citation support.\n\n2. **Internal contradictions**: Paragraph contradicts itself or contradicts other paragraphs in the document.\n\n3. **Equations inconsistent with cited sources**: Mathematical expressions, formulas, or models that do not match the notation, parameters, or structure in cited sources.\n\n4. **Terminology misuse**: Technical terms, definitions, or classifications used incorrectly relative to cited definitions or domain-standard meanings.\n\n5. **Chronological inconsistencies**: Dates, timelines, or temporal sequences that conflict with cited sources or known facts within the document.\n\n## Workflow-specific overrides\n\nEach workflow that uses this rule MUST specify inline, in its own workflow file:\n- **Target scope**: What is being audited (generated output vs. input documents under review).\n- **Evidence rule**: What evidence sources are acceptable for audit judgments.\n- **Fetch policy**: Whether new source fetching is allowed/required during audit.\n- **Token requirements**: Which citation token types are expected (`[KB:/WEB:/DOI:/ARXIV:/ZOTERO:]`).\n- **Audit semantics**: How `Confidence` and `Rationale` should be interpreted for that workflow (for example, veracity vs external sources, translation fidelity vs source text, coverage vs KB, etc.).\n\nFor current workflows, see in particular: `workflows/research.session.md`, `workflows/review.session.md`, `workflows/enhance.session.md`, and `workflows/translate.session.md`. These specializations MUST remain consistent with the base semantics defined in this file.\n\nAdditional design or analysis documents may exist in `docs/`, but the only normative contract for audit footnote structure and taxonomy is this file.\n","numLines":118,"startLine":1,"totalLines":118}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01T9Qt3SwDEZzFrVDhgVNhiB","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01WarPNML7dxCwJtJzgK8KGJ","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/averrik/Cloud/github/projects/AR-S2J2J.R/TITO/tasks/gap-tsf-blast-record-selection-metrics.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":35767,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":35767,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"478ab821-ce71-4d9a-9c86-7b54770e6fcb","uuid":"8eb6e86c-071b-44e6-b488-8bb6095dc7b6","request_id":"req_011CcmAyS76WeGEfrf7BGjQB"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WarPNML7dxCwJtJzgK8KGJ","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Gap: Blast Record Selection Metrics for TSF Scenario Analysis\n2\t\n3\tContext:\n4\t\n5\tWe are preparing an English technical report for project `AR-S2J2J.R`, which\n6\tselects and scales measured blasting records for TSF scenario analysis. The\n7\tpurpose of record selection is to represent dynamic demand relevant to saturated\n8\tfine tailings, pore-pressure accumulation, cyclic mobility, or liquefaction\n9\tpotential. The research gap is not generic definitions of PPV, PPA, or response\n10\tspectra; it is which record descriptors are physically defensible for this\n11\tmechanism.\n12\t\n13\tQuestions:\n14\t\n15\t1. Which intensity measures or record descriptors are supported in geotechnical\n16\t   dynamic or blast-vibration literature for evaluating pore-pressure\n17\t   accumulation or liquefaction-type response: component peak acceleration,\n18\t   component peak velocity, duration, significant cycles, dominant frequency,\n19\t   bandwidth, Arias intensity, cumulative absolute velocity, RMS measures,\n20\t   response spectra, Fourier spectra, or other metrics?\n21\t2. What formulas define the relevant metrics, and which assumptions or units\n22\t   matter when applying them to measured blast acceleration or velocity\n23\t   histories?\n24\t3. How should frequency content and duration be represented when selecting\n25\t   records for saturated fine tailings, where cumulative pore-pressure\n26\t   generation may depend on cycles and drainage rather than only peak amplitude?\n27\t4. Is there literature support for combining peak component demand with\n28\t   cumulative or frequency-content descriptors when selecting records for\n29\t   scenario analysis?\n30\t5. Which descriptors are weakly supported or potentially misleading for\n31\t   blast-induced TSF response, and what limitations should be stated?\n32\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"478ab821-ce71-4d9a-9c86-7b54770e6fcb","uuid":"fc7b538e-beaf-470a-ba91-9b201de473a9","timestamp":"2026-07-06T17:09:02.214Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/averrik/Cloud/github/projects/AR-S2J2J.R/TITO/tasks/gap-tsf-blast-record-selection-metrics.md","content":"# Gap: Blast Record Selection Metrics for TSF Scenario Analysis\n\nContext:\n\nWe are preparing an English technical report for project `AR-S2J2J.R`, which\nselects and scales measured blasting records for TSF scenario analysis. The\npurpose of record selection is to represent dynamic demand relevant to saturated\nfine tailings, pore-pressure accumulation, cyclic mobility, or liquefaction\npotential. The research gap is not generic definitions of PPV, PPA, or response\nspectra; it is which record descriptors are physically defensible for this\nmechanism.\n\nQuestions:\n\n1. Which intensity measures or record descriptors are supported in geotechnical\n   dynamic or blast-vibration literature for evaluating pore-pressure\n   accumulation or liquefaction-type response: component peak acceleration,\n   component peak velocity, duration, significant cycles, dominant frequency,\n   bandwidth, Arias intensity, cumulative absolute velocity, RMS measures,\n   response spectra, Fourier spectra, or other metrics?\n2. What formulas define the relevant metrics, and which assumptions or units\n   matter when applying them to measured blast acceleration or velocity\n   histories?\n3. How should frequency content and duration be represented when selecting\n   records for saturated fine tailings, where cumulative pore-pressure\n   generation may depend on cycles and drainage rather than only peak amplitude?\n4. Is there literature support for combining peak component demand with\n   cumulative or frequency-content descriptors when selecting records for\n   scenario analysis?\n5. Which descriptors are weakly supported or potentially misleading for\n   blast-induced TSF response, and what limitations should be stated?\n","numLines":32,"startLine":1,"totalLines":32}}}
